## Codebase Patterns

- Read `AGENTS.md` at the repo root first; it covers the three
  applications, the two typecheck configurations, and the test entries.
  Directory-level `AGENTS.md` files exist for `example-shared/` and
  `example-realistic-demo/`.
- Tests register themselves as a side effect and are invisible until
  imported from `test/matrix.ts` (ciphersuite fan-out) or `test/unit.ts`
  (everything else). Adding a file without adding the import silently
  tests nothing.
- Run one test file in about a second with esbuild plus a suite filter:
  `npx esbuild test/<file>.ts --bundle --platform=node --format=cjs
  --loader:.json=json --keep-names --outfile=.tmp.cjs && \
  MLS_SUITES=MLS_128_DHKEMX25519_AES128GCM_SHA256_Ed25519 node .tmp.cjs`
  Bundling only strips types, so typecheck separately.
- Zeroization has an ownership rule: `fill(0)` only what the current call
  allocated. `ClientState` is functional and node objects are shared
  across state versions, so wiping an input's buffer corrupts a state the
  caller still holds. Pass ownership explicitly when a function cannot
  tell (`ownsSecret` in `src/secret-tree.ts`).
- Any path secret that arrived from someone else (UpdatePath, Welcome)
  must be turned into keys with `toPrivateKeyPath(..., cs, tree)`. The
  fourth argument makes it verify each derived public key against the
  node's advertised `hpkePublicKey`. Only the local commit path
  (`create-commit.ts`) may omit it, since it derived both sides itself.
- Proposal validation inside `applyTreeMutations` (`src/client-state.ts`)
  must run against the accumulating tree, not the pre-mutation
  `ratchetTree` argument, or proposals in one commit never see each
  other. The `update` and `remove` branches still validate against the
  pre-mutation tree; only `add` was in scope for US-005.
- `validateLeafNodeCommon` is the commit-time counterpart of the
  join-time checks in `validateRatchetTree`. Any rule tightened in one
  belongs in the other, otherwise a commit builds a tree the library
  later refuses and every future join fails.
- `validateLeafNodeUpdateOrCommit` is the one entry point for every leaf
  *replacement*: Update proposals (via `applyTreeMutations`), a commit's
  UpdatePath leaf (via `process-messages.ts`), and join-time
  revalidation (via `validateRatchetTree`). All three call it against the
  tree as it stands *before* the new leaf is merged, so the node at
  `leafIndex` is the outgoing one -- except at join time, where the leaf
  under test is already in place.
- `AuthenticationService.validateCredential` takes an optional third
  argument, the credential being replaced. It is `undefined` for Adds,
  external joins at a blank leaf, `external_senders`, and GroupInfo
  signer checks.
- Credential-type support (RFC 9420 7.3) is pairwise and both halves live
  in `validateLeafNodeCommon`: every member must list the new leaf's
  credential type, and the new leaf must list every member's. Only the
  second half skips the leaf's own index, because there the outgoing leaf
  is the one being replaced.
- `package-lock.json` is committed and CI installs with `npm ci`, so a
  dependency change is not real until the lockfile is regenerated and
  committed with `package.json`. `npm audit` is at 0 vulnerabilities; the
  only runtime dependencies are `@hpke/*` and `@noble/*`, so findings are
  almost always dev-chain. `.github/workflows/auto-dependabot.yml` keeps
  `npm install` on purpose.
- A commit that removes the local client leaves its `ClientState` frozen
  at the old epoch -- `processCommit` returns early with the unchanged
  `groupContext` and only flips `groupActiveState`. Every epoch/groupId
  guard therefore still passes on that state, so
  `groupActiveState.kind === 'removedFromGroup'` is the only gate;
  `processPrivateMessage`/`processPublicMessage` check it first.
- Inside `applyProposals` (`src/client-state.ts`), `grouped` deliberately
  omits custom (numeric) proposal types, because they are never applied to
  the tree. Any rule that must also see custom proposals -- the
  `needsUpdatePath` decision, for one -- has to read `allProposals`.
- There are two places a path secret received from someone else is turned
  into a private key path, and they must stay in step:
  `updatePrivateKeyPath` (`src/process-messages.ts`, commit side) and
  `deriveWelcomePrivateKeyPath` (`src/private-key-path.ts`, Welcome side,
  called from `joinGroup`). Both walk the *committer's filtered direct
  path* via `pathSecretsAlongFilteredPath`, verify each derived key
  against the tree, and finish with `pruneBlankedNodes`. Never use
  `pathToRoot` for a received secret -- it walks to the geometric root via
  `findFirstNonBlankAncestor`, which can fold in a node the commit never
  rotated or store a key at a blank index. `pathToRoot` is now only for
  `getCommitSecret`.
- Adds alone carry no UpdatePath, so a group built purely from Add commits
  has blank parent nodes. A test that needs a fully populated tree has to
  follow the adds with empty commits from the relevant leaves.
- Both typechecks currently exit 0:
  `npx tsc -p tsconfig.json --noEmit` and
  `npx tsc -p example-realistic-demo/tsconfig.json --noEmit`.
  `npm run lint` exits 0. Check exit codes, not piped output.
- `npm test` is fully green as of US-024: 45321 tests, 0 failures, exit
  0. Entries before that one report a single failure in
  `test/example/how-to-use.ts` and call it pre-existing and unrelated;
  it was neither, and it is fixed. Do not inherit that excuse -- any
  failure you see now is one you introduced.
- A demo's dev-only gate is verified against the built asset, never
  against the source. `import.meta.env.DEV` is replaced with a literal
  `false` and the block is dropped; a `MODE` comparison is not, and
  `npm run build-example` builds with `--mode staging`. Build, then grep
  the output in `public/assets/` and
  `example-realistic-demo/public/assets/` for the assignment (`=`
  included -- the bare string appears in the `DevTools` copy either way).
- Markdown fences inside a bullet are indented to the bullet's text
  column. At column 0 the fence ends the list, which a diff will not
  show you. `npm run toc` rewrites the README table of contents in
  place; run it after touching a heading.
- Error class is part of the contract. `InternalError` says "this library
  has a bug"; anything reachable from a message a peer or attacker sends
  must be a `ValidationError`. When a low-level invariant guard (the
  `extendRatchetTree` throw in `src/ratchet-tree.ts` is the archetype)
  turns out to be remotely reachable, the fix is a `ValidationError` on
  the path that leads to it, not a softer message on the guard.
- The wire format admits exactly one protocol version (`mls10`), so a
  version mismatch is not constructible by driving the public API -- the
  encoders reject any other name. A test that needs one registers a
  temporary entry in the `protocolVersions` map and deletes it in a
  `finally`. See `test/validation/version-and-last-leaf.ts`.
- A `KeyPackage`'s `version` is covered by its key package ref, so a
  Welcome forged by hand is the only way to give a joiner a key package
  whose version differs from the group's. `extractWelcomeSecret` +
  `encryptGroupInfo` + `encryptGroupSecrets` are all exported and a
  random `joinerSecret` works, because the joiner only has to derive the
  same welcome key the test used. Pair it with a real signed GroupInfo
  from `createGroupInfoWithExternalPubAndRatchetTree` so the signature
  check still passes.

