## 2026-08-09 - US-001 (security-audit.md C1)

Stopped `src/secret-tree.ts` from zeroizing ratchet secrets that belong to
the caller's still-live `ClientState`.

- `createRatchetResultWithSecret` now takes an `ownsSecret` flag and wipes
  the consumed secret only when this call chain derived it. The send path
  (`consumeRatchet`) and the out-of-order receive path both pass `false`,
  because those secrets are the input tree's own buffers. The in-order
  receive path passes `senderData.generation > ratchet.generation`, which
  is true exactly when `ratchetUntil` allocated a fresh secret rather than
  returning its input.
- `removeOldGenerations` no longer wipes evicted generations. Every buffer
  in `unusedGenerations` was contributed by a prior `GenerationSecret`, so
  it is dropped for the garbage collector instead.

Files changed: `src/secret-tree.ts`, `test/validation/prior-state-reuse.ts`
(new), `test/validation/key-material-retention.ts`, `test/matrix.ts`,
`AGENTS.md`, `specs/prd.json`.

**Learnings for future iterations:**

- Four assertions in `test/validation/key-material-retention.ts` encoded
  the vulnerable behavior ("the superseded secret should be zeroized in
  place"). They were inverted to assert non-mutation rather than deleted;
  the accompanying `test(...)` titles and helper names had to change too,
  since the names described the old contract.
- The buffer a decrypt consumes is the *sender's* leaf in the receiver's
  tree, not the receiver's own leaf. A test probing decrypt-side aliasing
  that watches the receiver's own leaf watches the wrong buffer.
- The C1 bug partly hid itself: after a failed send both peers ratchet
  from all-zero secrets, so their derived keys still agree and the message
  round-trips. Only a peer whose state is still healthy exposes it, which
  is why the regression test decrypts the untampered original from a state
  that never saw the corrupt one.
- `CHANGELOG.md` is generated by `auto-changelog` from commit messages, so
  it is not hand-edited per story.
- `npm test` exits 1 on a pre-existing, unrelated failure:
  `test/example/how-to-use.ts`, "should render an item for each step".
  It calls `HowToUse({ steps: [] })` and expects `SETUP_STEPS.length`
  list items, so it assumes the component falls back to the defaults on
  an empty array while the sibling test asserts a supplied array is used
  verbatim. Verified failing on a clean tree with all of this story's
  changes stashed, so it is not a regression from US-001. It belongs to
  neither this story nor any other in `specs/prd.json`; whoever picks it
  up has to decide whether the fallback or the assertion is wrong.
  Everything else passes: 44487 tests, 44486 pass.

