## 2026-08-09 - US-005 (security-audit.md M4)

Made the commit-time key-uniqueness check for `Add` proposals as strict
as the join-time check, so a member can no longer commit a tree that
this library's own `validateRatchetTree` rejects.

- `applyTreeMutations` now validates each Add's KeyPackage against the
  tree accumulated so far rather than the pre-mutation `ratchetTree`.
  Two Adds in one commit are therefore compared to each other, and Adds
  are validated against a tree where the commit's Removes have already
  blanked the leaves they free (which is the order RFC 9420 applies
  them in anyway).
- `validateLeafNodeCommon` gained a parent-node comparison. The existing
  `keysAreNotUnique` check only looked at leaves, so an added leaf could
  carry a parent node's `hpkePublicKey`. It returns a distinct message,
  `hpke key matches an existing parent node key`, so a test can tell the
  two failures apart.

Files changed: `src/client-state.ts`,
`test/validation/add-key-uniqueness.ts` (new), `test/matrix.ts`,
`AGENTS.md`, `progress.log`, `specs/prd.json`.

**Learnings for future iterations:**

- Tampering a `KeyPackage` in place is useless for these tests: the leaf
  node signature is verified before any uniqueness check, so the test
  would pass on `CryptoVerificationError` and prove nothing. Re-mint the
  package instead -- `signLeafNodeKeyPackage` then `signKeyPackage`, both
  with the owner's own `privatePackage.signaturePrivateKey`. Keeping the
  attacker's real signature key is also what makes the test exercise the
  encryption-key comparison rather than the signature-key one.
- An add-only commit carries no UpdatePath, so the committer's parent
  nodes stay blank. To get a tree with populated parents, follow the
  batch of Adds with a second, empty `createCommit`, which does force a
  path. `CreateCommitOptions` has no flag to force one directly.
- `npm test` now reports 44663 tests with the same single pre-existing
  failure, `test/example/how-to-use.ts` "should render an item for each
  step" (unrelated to `src/`, see the US-001 entry).
- The root typecheck's one documented pre-existing error
  (`test/test-vectors/key-schedule.ts` TS2769) is gone; both configs now
  exit 0 with no errors. `AGENTS.md` was updated to match.

## 2026-08-09 - US-006 (security-audit.md M5, continuity half)

Gave `AuthenticationService.validateCredential` a third argument,
`priorCredential?:Credential`, so an application can enforce identity
continuity when a leaf is replaced. Before this the service saw only the
incoming credential, so member Bob could send an Update whose
`credential.identity` was `alice` and every peer would attribute Bob's
leaf to Alice from then on.

- `src/authentication-service.ts`: interface and
  `defaultAuthenticationService` take the optional third argument. The
  parameter is optional, so existing two-parameter implementations still
  satisfy the interface and behave exactly as before -- opting in means
  reading the new argument.
- `src/client-state.ts`: `validateLeafNodeCommon` forwards a
  `priorCredential`; `validateLeafNodeUpdateOrCommit` looks it up with a
  new `credentialAtLeaf(tree, leafIndex)` helper. Both the Update-proposal
  call site (`applyTreeMutations`) and the commit-UpdatePath call site
  (`process-messages.ts`) validate against the tree *before* the new leaf
  is merged, so the lookup returns the outgoing credential for free.
- README "Security Considerations" gained a third bullet with a worked
  `AuthenticationService` example and the exact list of when `prior` is
  and is not supplied. `CHANGELOG.md` gained an `Unreleased` section
  recording the signature change.

Files changed: `src/authentication-service.ts`, `src/client-state.ts`,
`test/validation/credential-continuity.ts` (new), `test/matrix.ts`,
`README.md`, `CHANGELOG.md`, `specs/prd.json`.

The reverse RFC 7.3 direction from the same audit finding -- requiring a
new leaf to support credential types already in use -- is US-007 and was
deliberately left alone.

**Learnings for future iterations:**

- `validateLeafNodeUpdateOrCommit` is the single entry point for *both*
  leaf-replacement directions (Update proposal and commit UpdatePath) and
  is also reused by `validateRatchetTree` at join time. At join time the
  leaf being validated is already in the tree, so `priorCredential` comes
  back equal to the credential under test. That self-comparison is
  harmless for a continuity check, but any future rule that must reject
  "prior equals new" would misfire there.
- There is no `signLeafNodeUpdate` export; only `signLeafNodeCommit` and
  `signLeafNodeKeyPackage`. To mint an `update`-source leaf in a test,
  build the TBS by hand and call `signWithLabel(key, 'LeafNodeTBS',
  encodeLeafNodeTBS(tbs), impl.signature)`.
- A hand-built Update leaf needs a *fresh* `hpkePublicKey` (generate a
  throwaway KeyPackage for it), or `validateLeafNodeCommon`'s
  key-uniqueness check rejects it for an unrelated reason and the test
  passes vacuously. Its `signaturePublicKey` may stay the same, since the
  uniqueness scan skips the leaf's own index.
- `createProposal` returns `message:MLSMessage`, a union that includes
  Welcome. `processMessage` takes the narrower public/private union, so a
  test has to narrow on `message.wireformat` before passing it along --
  the existing scenario tests all do this.
- Proposal leaf nodes are *not* validated at receipt (`processProposal`
  only records them in `unappliedProposals`); validation happens when
  someone commits. A test for proposal validation must therefore drive it
  through `createCommit` or `processMessage` on a commit.
- `CHANGELOG.md` is regenerated by `auto-changelog` on `npm version`, so
  the hand-written `Unreleased` section will be replaced at release time
  by the commit-derived list. It is there to satisfy the story's
  documentation criterion in the meantime.
- `npm test` still exits 1 on the same pre-existing, unrelated failure:
  `test/example/how-to-use.ts`, "should render an item for each step".
  44679 tests, 44678 pass.

