## 2026-08-09 - US-007 (security-audit.md M5, capability half)

Closed the reverse RFC 9420 7.3 direction that US-006 deliberately left
open: a new or replacement leaf must now itself support every credential
type already in use in the group.

- `validateLeafNodeCommon` (`src/client-state.ts`) gained a second
  credential scan next to the existing one. The old check asked "does
  every member support this leaf's credential type"; the new one asks
  "does this leaf support every member's credential type", and returns
  `ValidationError('LeafNode does not support a credential type in use by
  a member of the group')`. Because the check sits in the shared helper it
  covers all three entry points at once: Adds (via
  `validateLeafNodeKeyPackage`, which passes `leafIndex: undefined`),
  Update proposals and commit UpdatePath leaves (via
  `validateLeafNodeUpdateOrCommit`), and join-time revalidation (via
  `validateRatchetTree`).
- The new scan skips the leaf's own index; the old one does not. That
  asymmetry is deliberate -- at `leafIndex` sits the leaf being replaced,
  which will not be a member once the commit applies, and the leaf's own
  credential type is already covered by the first check, which does
  include its index.

Files changed: `src/client-state.ts`,
`test/validation/credential-capability-floor.ts` (new), `test/matrix.ts`,
`AGENTS.md`, `progress.log`, `specs/prd.json`.

**Learnings for future iterations:**

- Verified red first: pre-fix, both the replacement leaf and the Add were
  accepted with no error (1 of 3 assertions passing, and the one that
  passed was the accept-the-good-leaf control). Post-fix all 3 pass.
- That control assertion is what makes the test worth anything. Bob's
  narrowed leaf and Bob's full leaf are identical apart from the
  capability list, so if the narrowed one were rejected for some
  unrelated reason (stale hpke key, bad signature) the full one would be
  rejected too and the test would fail loudly rather than pass vacuously.
- `defaultCapabilities()` advertises `basic` and `x509` plus grease, and
  every test identity uses `basic`. Filtering just `basic` out of a copy
  is therefore the minimal way to build a leaf that violates only this
  rule. Adding a new credential type to the library without also adding
  it to `defaultCapabilities` would now make the first leaf that uses it
  unaddable.
- The RFC test vectors are unaffected: `passive-client-welcome`,
  `passive-client-handling-commit` and `passive-client-random` all still
  pass, which is the evidence that the new rule does not reject
  conformant trees.
- `npm test` still exits 1 on the same pre-existing, unrelated failure:
  `test/example/how-to-use.ts`, "should render an item for each step".
  44691 tests, 44690 pass. `npm run lint` and both typechecks exit 0.

