## 2026-08-09 - US-009 (security-audit.md H3)

Bounded the demo Worker's join requests, the one write a complete
stranger can cause.

- `classifyJoinRequest` in `example-realistic-demo/room-logic.ts` is the
  new rule, pure and unit tested: a 16 KiB ceiling on the key package, a
  cap of 64 distinct pending identities, and a 1000 ms per-socket
  interval. Precedence is size, then rate limit, then cap -- size first
  because it is the one refusal that waiting will never resolve.
- The cap counts rows rather than requests. `pending` is keyed by
  identity, so a repeat request from a queued identity replaces its own
  row and is let through a full queue; refusing it would strand a
  requester whose key package changed behind a queue they are already in.
- `onJoinRequest` in `index.ts` consults it and answers the verdict as
  the error reason. A refusal writes nothing -- not the row, not the
  attachment, not the throttle -- so a refused request cannot itself be
  the storage growth the limits exist to stop.
- The throttle lives in `SocketState` (`lastJoinRequestAt`), so `attach`
  now carries it across every rewrite. Without that, `hello` resets it
  and one `hello` per request is the bypass. `readAttachment` reads it
  the same way it reads `isCreator`: a missing or non-finite value
  becomes null rather than flowing into a comparison.
- Three new `ErrorReason` values: `key-package-too-large`,
  `too-many-pending`, `rate-limited`. Kept apart rather than folded into
  one refusal because they mean different things to the requester. The
  client's `connection.ts` prints any reason verbatim, so it needed no
  change.

Files changed: `example-realistic-demo/room-logic.ts`,
`example-realistic-demo/protocol.ts`, `example-realistic-demo/index.ts`,
`example-realistic-demo/scripts/probe.mjs`,
`example-realistic-demo/AGENTS.md`,
`test/example-realistic-demo/room-logic.ts`,
`test/example-realistic-demo/protocol.ts`, `specs/prd.json`.

**Learnings for future iterations:**

- Rate limiting in this Worker has to be socket-scoped, not
  identity-scoped. Identities are free to mint, so a per-identity limit
  throttles nobody -- and it would also break probe checks 12 and 13,
  which re-request for identity B within a second of each other from
  fresh sockets.
- Anything stored in `SocketState` that must not be resettable by the
  client has to be carried over inside `attach`, which otherwise rebuilds
  the attachment from scratch on every `hello` and every accepted join
  request.
- The probe is the only thing that exercises `index.ts`. Running it takes
  a build plus a Worker: `npm run build:realistic`, then
  `npm run worker:realistic` in the background, then
  `node example-realistic-demo/scripts/probe.mjs`, then kill the Worker.
  All 25 checks pass; 24 and 25 are new.
- `npm test` still exits 1 on the same pre-existing, unrelated failure:
  `test/example/how-to-use.ts`, "should render an item for each step".
  44734 tests, 44733 pass. `npm run lint` and both typechecks exit 0.

