## 2026-08-09 - US-010 (security-audit.md M1)

Length-bounded every client-supplied string on the realistic demo's wire.

- `example-realistic-demo/protocol.ts` now exports `MAX_IDENTITY_LENGTH`
  (512), `MAX_CREATOR_TOKEN_LENGTH` (128), `MAX_PAYLOAD_LENGTH` (256 KiB)
  and `MAX_WIRE_MESSAGE_LENGTH` (320 KiB), each with the reasoning for
  its size documented beside it. Two private helpers, `isIdentity` and
  `isPayload`, replace the bare `isStr` in `isClientMessage`,
  `isLogEntry`, `isPendingRequest` and `isRoomMessage`, so a string is
  bounded wherever it appears rather than only on ingest.
- `index.ts` checks `raw.length` against `MAX_WIRE_MESSAGE_LENGTH`
  *before* `JSON.parse`. The per-field bounds only apply to a frame that
  has already been parsed, and parsing a multi-megabyte frame is itself
  the cost worth refusing. An oversized frame is answered `bad-message`
  and nothing is persisted.
- Probe check 26 (new) sends an oversized payload and an oversized frame
  from the creator socket -- the one socket where no other gate can
  refuse -- asserts two `bad-message` answers, asserts neither reached
  the log, then sends a payload at exactly the limit and requires it to
  be accepted.

Interpretation of the acceptance criteria: `name` has no bound of its own
because it has no field of its own. Identity on this wire is always the
signature public key; a display name rides inside a key package as a
basic credential, so it is bounded by whatever bounds the key package.
That is recorded in the doc block next to the constants.

Files changed: `example-realistic-demo/protocol.ts`,
`example-realistic-demo/index.ts`,
`example-realistic-demo/scripts/probe.mjs`,
`example-realistic-demo/AGENTS.md`,
`test/example-realistic-demo/protocol.ts`, `specs/prd.json`.

**Learnings for future iterations:**

- A join request's key package is bounded twice on purpose, and the two
  bounds must not be made equal. `protocol.ts` gives it the loose
  `MAX_PAYLOAD_LENGTH` structural wall; `MAX_KEY_PACKAGE_LENGTH` (16 KiB)
  in `room-logic.ts` is the real rule. Tightening the structural one to
  match would turn the named `key-package-too-large` refusal into a bare
  `bad-message` and break probe check 24. The general shape: a structural
  guard should be the outer wall, and the specific handler rule should be
  what a client actually hits, so refusals stay legible.
- The frame wall belongs before `JSON.parse`, not after. Every bound in
  `protocol.ts` is unreachable until the frame is already parsed, so the
  parse itself is the only cost a per-field bound cannot avoid.
- `MAX_WIRE_MESSAGE_LENGTH > MAX_PAYLOAD_LENGTH` is load bearing and has
  its own test. Set the frame wall at or below the payload bound and a
  legal maximum-size payload can never fit inside a legal frame, which
  fails only for the largest messages and so would not show up in
  ordinary use.
- Probe checks that need a socket no other gate refuses should use the
  creator. Check 20's stranger/removed sockets are refused `not-member`
  before a size rule is ever consulted, so an oversized-write check
  written against them would pass with the bound deleted.
- `npm test` still exits 1 on the same pre-existing, unrelated failure:
  `test/example/how-to-use.ts`, "should render an item for each step".

