## 2026-08-09 - US-011 (security-audit.md M6)

Flipped `defaultLifetimeConfig.validateLifetimeOnReceive` from `false` to
`true`, so a KeyPackage leaf node arriving from a peer has its
`notBefore`/`notAfter` window and its total span checked against the local
clock instead of being accepted unconditionally.

- `src/lifetime-config.ts`: default is now `true`; the docstring describes
  the enforced default and frames `false` as the explicit opt-out (bad
  local clock, interop with peers publishing stale KeyPackages). No
  production code besides the constant changed -- `validateLeafNodeKeyPackage`
  already read the flag on the receive path.
- `test/validation/lifetime-on-receive.ts` (new, registered in
  `test/matrix.ts`): Alice adds Charlie while Charlie's KeyPackage is still
  inside a one-hour window, then `Date.now` is stubbed past `notAfter` and
  Bob processes the commit. Under the default Bob throws
  `ValidationError('Current time not within Lifetime')`; with a
  `ClientConfig` carrying `validateLifetimeOnReceive: false` the same
  commit is accepted. A non-ciphersuite assertion pins the default value
  itself.
- `test/test-vectors/passive-client-scenarios.ts`: the RFC vectors ship
  fixed KeyPackages whose lifetimes expired long ago, so `joinGroup` there
  now passes a `ClientConfig` that opts out explicitly. This was the only
  existing test that depended on the old default.
- Docs: README "Security Considerations" (the lifetime bullet moved out of
  the "must be addressed before shipping" list into a new "Two more
  defaults worth knowing about" list, since it is no longer a permissive
  default), `docs/GLOSSARY.md`, and a `### Changed` entry in
  `CHANGELOG.md` flagging the behavior change and the opt-out snippet.

Files changed: `src/lifetime-config.ts`,
`test/validation/lifetime-on-receive.ts` (new), `test/matrix.ts`,
`test/test-vectors/passive-client-scenarios.ts`, `README.md`,
`docs/GLOSSARY.md`, `CHANGELOG.md`, `specs/prd.json`.

**Learnings for future iterations:**

- A commit's own author can never carry an expired KeyPackage past
  `validateLeafNodeKeyPackage`: `sentByClient` is hardcoded `true` in
  `createCommit` (via `applyProposals`) and `false` in `processCommit`, and
  the lifetime branch is `sentByClient || config.validateLifetimeOnReceive`.
  So a test for the receive path cannot simply mint an already-expired
  KeyPackage -- commit it while valid, then stub `Date.now` before the
  receiving member processes the message.
- `joinGroup`'s `clientConfig` is the 8th parameter, behind optional
  `ratchetTree` and `resumingFromState`; both must be passed (`undefined`
  is fine for the latter) to reach it.
- `validateRatchetTree` runs `validateLeafNodeKeyPackage` over every leaf
  still sourced from `key_package`, so any tightening of lifetime rules
  bites at join time as well as at commit time. That is what broke the RFC
  test vectors, whose recorded lifetimes are years stale -- expect any
  future clock-sensitive rule to need the same explicit opt-out there.
- `defaultLifetime()` spans 2595600 seconds (now-3600 to now+2592000),
  comfortably under the 2628000-second `maximumTotalLifetime`, so the
  stricter default does not reject the library's own key packages.
- `npm test` still exits 1 on the same pre-existing, unrelated failure:
  `test/example/how-to-use.ts`, "should render an item for each step".
  Everything else passes: 44812 of 44813.
