## 2026-08-09 - US-012 (security-audit.md M7)

Removed the accept-all `AuthenticationService` from the default path, so
credential validation is now a decision the application has to make.

- `src/authentication-service.ts` replaces `defaultAuthenticationService`
  with two named exports. `failClosedAuthenticationService` throws a
  `UsageError` naming the opt-in; `unsafeAcceptAllAuthenticationService`
  is the old accept-all behavior. `defaultClientConfig.authService` is the
  fail-closed one.
- Approach chosen: fail closed *and* keep the accept-all reachable under
  an unmistakable name. Either half alone leaves a gap -- a fail-closed
  default with no named escape hatch pushes people to write their own
  `async () => true`, which is invisible to a reviewer, and renaming
  alone still leaves whatever `defaultClientConfig` carries as the silent
  answer. Throwing rather than returning `false` is deliberate: `false`
  surfaces as `ValidationError('Could not validate credential')`, which
  reads like a rejected peer rather than a missing configuration.
- `src/resumption.ts` was passing no config at all, so `reinit`/`branch`
  reset the resumed group to library defaults and dropped whatever the
  application had configured. All four helpers now carry
  `state.clientConfig` across.
- `src/index.ts` now also exports `defaultClientConfig`; without it a
  consumer cannot spread the defaults to fill in `authService`.

Files changed: `src/authentication-service.ts`, `src/client-config.ts`,
`src/index.ts`, `src/resumption.ts`, `example-shared/demo-client-config.ts`
(new), `example-shared/persistence-storage.ts`, `example/demo-actions.ts`,
`example-realistic-demo/client/mls-actions.ts`,
`test/helpers/client-config.ts` (new),
`test/validation/auth-service-opt-in.ts` (new), `test/matrix.ts`, 55 test
files under `test/scenario/` and `test/validation/`, `README.md`,
`CHANGELOG.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `example-shared/AGENTS.md`,
`specs/prd.json`.

**Learnings for future iterations:**

- The `ClientConfig` reaches the library at exactly three entry points --
  `createGroup`, `joinGroup`, `joinGroupExternal` -- and then travels
  inside `ClientState.clientConfig`. Everything downstream
  (`createCommit`, `processMessage`, the resumption helpers) reads it off
  the state, so a new config field needs no new plumbing, only those
  three call sites.
- Widening a config default is a ~140-call-site edit across
  `test/scenario` and `test/validation`. Each config argument is
  positional and trails optional parameters, so the mechanical form is
  "count the top-level arguments, pad with `undefined`". A short script
  that walks balanced parens is far safer than editing by hand; watch for
  argument lists with a trailing comma, which produce `,,` if the new
  argument is appended blindly.
- `test/helpers/client-config.ts` now holds `testClientConfig` -- the
  defaults plus the accept-all service. Any new test that builds or joins
  a group needs it, or it dies at the first Add with a `UsageError`
  rather than a validation failure.
- The demos share `example-shared/demo-client-config.ts`. It has a second
  reader: `freshClientConfig` in `example-shared/persistence-storage.ts`
  rebuilds the stripped config on restore, so a demo config change that
  misses that function silently restores sessions under different rules.
- `npm test` still exits 1 on the same pre-existing, unrelated failure in
  `test/example/how-to-use.ts`, "should render an item for each step".

