## 2026-08-09 - US-015 (security-audit.md L1, L2)

Made every AEAD key/nonce wipe run on the error path, and added the two
sender-data wipes that were missing entirely.

- `src/message-protection.ts`: the content key/nonce wipes in `protect`
  and `unprotectPrivateMessage` moved into a `finally` around the AEAD
  call. Both buffers come from `consumeRatchet` / `ratchetToGeneration`,
  which derive them fresh for the call, so the US-001 ownership rule is
  satisfied.
- `src/private-message.ts`: `encryptSenderData` and `decryptSenderData`
  now wipe their key and nonce, also in a `finally`. Both are expanded
  locally from `senderDataSecret` via `expandWithLabel`, so nothing the
  caller owns is touched.
- `test/validation/aead-key-zeroization.ts` (new, registered in
  `test/matrix.ts`) wraps `cs.hpke.encryptAead` / `decryptAead` in a spy
  that records the buffers it was handed and can reject on a chosen call
  index. Six tests per ciphersuite: content-key wipe on the protect and
  unprotect error paths, and sender-data wipes on the success and error
  paths of both directions. All 12 assertions failed before the fix.

Files changed: `src/message-protection.ts`, `src/private-message.ts`,
`test/validation/aead-key-zeroization.ts` (new), `test/matrix.ts`,
`test/validation/key-material-retention.ts` (comments), `AGENTS.md`,
`specs/prd.json`.

**Learnings for future iterations:**

- Spying on `cs.hpke` is the cheap way to observe key material a function
  never returns: spread the impl, spread `impl.hpke`, override the one
  method, and cast the result to `CiphersuiteImpl`. The recorded
  `Uint8Array` is the same object the callee wipes, so asserting
  `every(b => b === 0)` after the call reads the real buffer.
- Call order distinguishes the two AEAD sites without any extra
  plumbing. `protect` encrypts content first, then sender data;
  `unprotect` decrypts sender data first, then content. Two existing
  tests in `key-material-retention.ts` already relied on this and their
  comments called the sender-data half "out of scope" -- those comments
  were updated rather than left to rot.
- A wipe placed after an `await` is a wipe that a forged ciphertext skips.
  Any `fill(0)` following an AEAD call is a latent finding; `finally` is
  the only correct placement.
- Full suite: 45053 tests, 45052 pass, 1 fail -- still the same
  pre-existing, unrelated `test/example/how-to-use.ts` "should render an
  item for each step". Both typechecks and `npm run lint` exit 0.
