## 2026-08-09 - US-020
- Bounds-checked `decodeReuseGuard` (`src/sender.ts`). It used
  `subarray(offset, offset + 4)`, which clamps silently: a truncated
  buffer produced a 1-3 byte value branded as a length-4 `ReuseGuard`
  while reporting 4 bytes consumed. It now returns `undefined` (the
  decoder failure signal) when the offset is negative or fewer than 4
  bytes remain.
- Tests added to `test/codec/sender-data.ts` (already imported from
  `test/unit.ts`): short buffers at every length 0-3, offset handling
  including the exact-fit case, and `decodeSenderData` over every
  truncation of an encoded `SenderData` from 8 bytes up.
- Files changed: `src/sender.ts`, `test/codec/sender-data.ts`,
  `specs/prd.json`, `progress.log`.
- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - `Decoder<T>` in `src/codec/tls-decoder.ts` is
    `(b, offset) => [T, number] | undefined`; failure is `undefined`,
    never a throw. `mapDecoders` short-circuits on `undefined`, so a
    single leaf decoder returning `undefined` fails the whole struct --
    no extra plumbing needed at the call site.
  - The numeric decoders in `src/codec/number.ts` get their bounds check
    for free because `DataView.getUintN` throws past the end and they
    catch it. Anything built on `subarray` has no such backstop and must
    check `offset + n > b.length` explicitly. Worth auditing any other
    `subarray`-based decoder the same way.
  - Full suite: 45269 tests, 45268 pass, 1 fail -- still the same
    pre-existing, unrelated `test/example/how-to-use.ts` "should render
    an item for each step". `npx tsc --noEmit` reports zero errors and
    `npm run lint` exits 0.
