## 2026-08-09 - US-022
- Documented what each padding mode actually hides. `PaddingConfig`,
  `defaultPaddingConfig`, and `byteLengthToPad` in `src/padding-config.ts`
  now carry docstrings that say plainly: `padUntilLength` hides length
  only *below* its floor (at or above it, nothing is padded and the
  length is visible up to constant framing overhead), and `alwaysPad`
  hides no length at all, because ciphertext length stays an affine
  function of plaintext length and an observer just subtracts the
  constant. Both notes say that neither mode hides anything but length:
  epoch, content type, and message timing/rate are always on the wire.
- README `Security Considerations` gained a matching bullet ("Two more
  defaults" became "Three more defaults"), and the `PaddingConfig` entry
  in `docs/GLOSSARY.md` was tightened to match.
- Reflowed `defaultPaddingConfig` and `byteLengthToPad` to fit 80
  columns; behavior is byte-for-byte identical (the `else return` became
  a plain `return` after an early return).
- Files changed: `src/padding-config.ts`, `README.md`,
  `docs/GLOSSARY.md`, `specs/prd.json`, `progress.log`.
- Per execution rule 3 this is a documentation story, so no new test was
  written: there is no behavior change to capture. `test/codec/padding.ts`
  (9 assertions) was run to prove the reflow left `byteLengthToPad`
  intact.
- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - Padding config lives in exactly three places that must agree:
    `src/padding-config.ts`, the README `Security Considerations`
    section, and the `PaddingConfig` / `Padding` entries in
    `docs/GLOSSARY.md`. The README bullet list there is prefixed by a
    counted lead-in ("Three more defaults worth knowing about"), so
    adding a bullet means editing the count.
  - Full suite: 45307 tests, 45306 pass, 1 fail -- still the same
    pre-existing, unrelated `test/example/how-to-use.ts` "should render
    an item for each step". `npm run lint` and
    `npx tsc -p tsconfig.json --noEmit` both exit 0.
